elements {
  type: TYPEDEF
  name: "constant_buffer"
  brief: "Constant buffer"
  description: "A mutable collection of shader constants created with\n[ref:render.constant_buffer]. Assign constants by name using [type:vector4]\nor [type:matrix4] values, or arrays of those values, then pass the buffer in\nthe `constants` option to [ref:render.draw]. Constant buffers are Lua\nuserdata and cannot be iterated with `pairs()` or `ipairs()`."
  parameters {
    name: "value"
    doc: "shader constant buffer"
    types: "userdata"
    is_optional: false
  }
  examples: "```lua\nlocal constants = render.constant_buffer()\nconstants.tint = vmath.vector4(1, 0.5, 0.5, 1)\nrender.draw(self.model_predicate, { constants = constants })\n```"
  language: ""
}
elements {
  type: TYPEDEF
  name: "render_target"
  brief: "Render target"
  description: "An opaque graphics handle identifying an off-screen render target. Create\none with [ref:render.render_target], draw into it with\n[ref:render.set_render_target], and release dynamically created targets with\n[ref:render.delete_render_target]. A render-target resource handle can also\nbe obtained from [ref:resource.get_render_target_info]."
  parameters {
    name: "value"
    doc: "opaque render-target handle"
    types: "number"
    is_optional: false
  }
  examples: "```lua\nfunction init(self)\n    local color_params = {\n        format = graphics.TEXTURE_FORMAT_RGBA,\n        width = 320,\n        height = 180,\n    }\n    self.target = render.render_target({\n        [graphics.BUFFER_TYPE_COLOR0_BIT] = color_params,\n    })\nend\n\nfunction update(self)\n    if not self.target then\n        return\n    end\n    render.set_render_target(self.target)\n    -- Draw off-screen content here.\n    render.set_render_target(render.RENDER_TARGET_DEFAULT)\nend\n\nfunction on_message(self, message_id)\n    if message_id == hash(\"release_render_target\") and self.target then\n        render.delete_render_target(self.target)\n        self.target = nil\n    end\nend\n```"
  language: ""
}
elements {
  type: TYPEDEF
  name: "texture"
  brief: "Texture handle"
  description: "An opaque graphics handle identifying a texture. Texture handles are\nreturned by APIs such as [ref:resource.get_texture_info],\n[ref:material.get_textures], and [ref:compute.get_textures]. Pass a handle\nto [ref:render.enable_texture] to bind the texture in a render script. The\nresource or render target that owns the texture controls its lifetime."
  parameters {
    name: "value"
    doc: "opaque texture handle"
    types: "number"
    is_optional: false
  }
  examples: "```lua\nlocal texture_info = resource.get_texture_info(\"/assets/logo.texturec\")\nlocal texture_handle = texture_info.handle\nrender.enable_texture(\"texture_sampler\", texture_handle)\n```"
  language: ""
}
elements {
  type: TYPEDEF
  name: "render_predicate"
  brief: "Material-tag render filter"
  description: "An opaque filter that selects renderable objects by material tag. Create a\npredicate with [ref:render.predicate] and pass it to [ref:render.draw]. When\nmultiple tags are supplied, an object\'s material must contain all of them.\nPredicates are intended for use in render scripts."
  parameters {
    name: "value"
    doc: "material-tag render filter"
    types: "userdata"
    is_optional: false
  }
  examples: "```lua\nlocal opaque = render.predicate({ \"opaque\" })\nrender.draw(opaque)\n```"
  language: ""
}
elements {
  type: FUNCTION
  name: "render.constant_buffer"
  brief: "create a new constant buffer."
  description: "Constant buffers are used to set shader program variables and are optionally passed to the `render.draw()` function.\nThe buffer\'s constant elements can be indexed like an ordinary Lua table, but you can\'t iterate over them with pairs() or ipairs()."
  returnvalues {
    name: "buffer"
    doc: "new constant buffer"
    types: "constant_buffer"
  }
  examples: "Set a \"tint\" constant in a constant buffer in the render script:\n\n```lua\nlocal constants = render.constant_buffer()\nconstants.tint = vmath.vector4(1, 1, 1, 1)\n```\n\nThen use the constant buffer when drawing a predicate:\n\n```lua\nrender.draw(self.my_pred, {constants = constants})\n```\n\nThe constant buffer also supports array values by specifying constants in a table:\n\n```lua\nlocal constants = render.constant_buffer()\nconstants.light_colors    = {}\nconstants.light_colors[1] = vmath.vector4(1, 0, 0, 1)\nconstants.light_colors[2] = vmath.vector4(0, 1, 0, 1)\nconstants.light_colors[3] = vmath.vector4(0, 0, 1, 1)\n```\n\nYou can also create the table by passing the vectors directly when creating the table:\n\n```lua\nlocal constants = render.constant_buffer()\nconstants.light_colors    = {\n     vmath.vector4(1, 0, 0, 1)\n     vmath.vector4(0, 1, 0, 1)\n     vmath.vector4(0, 0, 1, 1)\n}\n\n-- Add more constant to the array\nconstants.light_colors[4] = vmath.vector4(1, 1, 1, 1)\n```"
  language: ""
}
elements {
  type: FUNCTION
  name: "render.enable_state"
  brief: "enables a render state"
  description: "Enables a particular render state. The state will be enabled until disabled."
  parameters {
    name: "state"
    doc: "state to enable\n\n- `graphics.STATE_DEPTH_TEST`\n- `graphics.STATE_STENCIL_TEST`\n- `graphics.STATE_BLEND`\n- `graphics.STATE_ALPHA_TEST` ([icon:iOS][icon:android] not available on iOS and Android)\n- `graphics.STATE_CULL_FACE`\n- `graphics.STATE_POLYGON_OFFSET_FILL`"
    types: "graphics.STATE"
    is_optional: false
  }
  examples: "Enable stencil test when drawing the gui predicate, then disable it:\n\n```lua\nrender.enable_state(graphics.STATE_STENCIL_TEST)\nrender.draw(self.gui_pred)\nrender.disable_state(graphics.STATE_STENCIL_TEST)\n```"
  language: ""
}
elements {
  type: FUNCTION
  name: "render.disable_state"
  brief: "disables a render state"
  description: "Disables a render state."
  parameters {
    name: "state"
    doc: "state to disable\n\n- `graphics.STATE_DEPTH_TEST`\n- `graphics.STATE_STENCIL_TEST`\n- `graphics.STATE_BLEND`\n- `graphics.STATE_ALPHA_TEST` ([icon:iOS][icon:android] not available on iOS and Android)\n- `graphics.STATE_CULL_FACE`\n- `graphics.STATE_POLYGON_OFFSET_FILL`"
    types: "graphics.STATE"
    is_optional: false
  }
  examples: "Disable face culling when drawing the tile predicate:\n\n```lua\nrender.disable_state(graphics.STATE_CULL_FACE)\nrender.draw(self.tile_pred)\n```"
  language: ""
}
elements {
  type: FUNCTION
  name: "render.set_viewport"
  brief: "sets the render viewport"
  description: "Set the render viewport to the specified rectangle."
  parameters {
    name: "x"
    doc: "left corner"
    types: "integer"
    is_optional: false
  }
  parameters {
    name: "y"
    doc: "bottom corner"
    types: "integer"
    is_optional: false
  }
  parameters {
    name: "width"
    doc: "viewport width"
    types: "integer"
    is_optional: false
  }
  parameters {
    name: "height"
    doc: "viewport height"
    types: "integer"
    is_optional: false
  }
  examples: "```lua\n-- Set the viewport to the window dimensions.\nrender.set_viewport(0, 0, render.get_window_width(), render.get_window_height())\n```"
  language: ""
}
elements {
  type: FUNCTION
  name: "render.render_target"
  brief: "creates a new render target"
  description: "Creates a new render target according to the supplied\nspecification table.\n\nThe render target can be created to support multiple color attachments. Each attachment can have different format settings and texture filters,\nbut attachments must be added in sequence, meaning you cannot create a render target at slot 0 and 3.\nInstead it has to be created with all four buffer types ranging from [0..3] (as denoted by graphics.BUFFER_TYPE_COLORX_BIT where \'X\' is the attachment you want to create).\nIt is not guaranteed that the device running the script can support creating render targets with multiple color attachments. To check if the device can support multiple attachments,\nyou can check if the `render` table contains any of the `BUFFER_TYPE_COLOR1_BIT`, `BUFFER_TYPE_COLOR2_BIT` or `BUFFER_TYPE_COLOR3_BIT` constants:\n\n```lua\nfunction init(self)\n    if graphics.BUFFER_TYPE_COLOR1_BIT == nil then\n        -- this devices does not support multiple color attachments\n    end\nend\n```"
  returnvalues {
    name: "render_target"
    doc: "new render target"
    types: "render_target"
  }
  parameters {
    name: "parameters"
    doc: "render-target parameters"
    types: "render.render_target_params"
    is_optional: false
  }
  examples: "How to create a new render target and draw to it:\n\n```lua\nfunction init(self)\n    -- render target buffer parameters\n    local color_params = { format = graphics.TEXTURE_FORMAT_RGBA,\n                           width = render.get_window_width(),\n                           height = render.get_window_height(),\n                           min_filter = graphics.TEXTURE_FILTER_LINEAR,\n                           mag_filter = graphics.TEXTURE_FILTER_LINEAR,\n                           u_wrap = graphics.TEXTURE_WRAP_CLAMP_TO_EDGE,\n                           v_wrap = graphics.TEXTURE_WRAP_CLAMP_TO_EDGE }\n    local depth_params = { format = graphics.TEXTURE_FORMAT_DEPTH,\n                           width = render.get_window_width(),\n                           height = render.get_window_height(),\n                           u_wrap = graphics.TEXTURE_WRAP_CLAMP_TO_EDGE,\n                           v_wrap = graphics.TEXTURE_WRAP_CLAMP_TO_EDGE }\n    self.my_render_target = render.render_target({sample_count = 4, [graphics.BUFFER_TYPE_COLOR0_BIT] = color_params, [graphics.BUFFER_TYPE_DEPTH_BIT] = depth_params })\nend\n\nfunction update(self, dt)\n    -- enable target so all drawing is done to it\n    render.set_render_target(self.my_render_target)\n\n    -- draw a predicate to the render target\n    render.draw(self.my_pred)\nend\n```\n\nHow to create a render target with multiple outputs:\n\n```lua\nfunction init(self)\n    -- render target buffer parameters\n    local color_params_rgba = { format = graphics.TEXTURE_FORMAT_RGBA,\n                                width = render.get_window_width(),\n                                height = render.get_window_height(),\n                                min_filter = graphics.TEXTURE_FILTER_LINEAR,\n                                mag_filter = graphics.TEXTURE_FILTER_LINEAR,\n                                u_wrap = graphics.TEXTURE_WRAP_CLAMP_TO_EDGE,\n                                v_wrap = graphics.TEXTURE_WRAP_CLAMP_TO_EDGE }\n    local color_params_float = { format = graphics.TEXTURE_FORMAT_RG32F,\n                           width = render.get_window_width(),\n                           height = render.get_window_height(),\n                           min_filter = graphics.TEXTURE_FILTER_LINEAR,\n                           mag_filter = graphics.TEXTURE_FILTER_LINEAR,\n                           u_wrap = graphics.TEXTURE_WRAP_CLAMP_TO_EDGE,\n                           v_wrap = graphics.TEXTURE_WRAP_CLAMP_TO_EDGE }\n\n\n    -- Create a render target with three color attachments\n    -- Note: No depth buffer is attached here\n    self.my_render_target = render.render_target({\n           [graphics.BUFFER_TYPE_COLOR0_BIT] = color_params_rgba,\n           [graphics.BUFFER_TYPE_COLOR1_BIT] = color_params_rgba,\n           [graphics.BUFFER_TYPE_COLOR2_BIT] = color_params_float, })\nend\n\nfunction update(self, dt)\n    -- set target so all drawing is done to it\n    render.set_render_target(self.my_render_target)\n\n    -- draw a predicate to the render target\n    render.draw(self.my_pred)\nend\n```"
  language: ""
}
elements {
  type: FUNCTION
  name: "render.delete_render_target"
  brief: "deletes a render target"
  description: "Deletes a render target created by a render script.\nYou cannot delete a render target resource."
  parameters {
    name: "render_target"
    doc: "render target to delete"
    types: "render_target"
    is_optional: false
  }
  examples: "How to delete a render target:\n\n```lua\n render.delete_render_target(self.my_render_target)\n```"
  language: ""
}
elements {
  type: CONSTANT
  name: "render.RENDER_TARGET_DEFAULT"
  brief: ""
  description: ""
  parameters {
    name: "value"
    doc: ""
    types: "render_target"
  }
  examples: ""
  language: ""
}
elements {
  type: FUNCTION
  name: "render.set_render_target"
  brief: "sets a render target"
  description: "Sets a render target. Subsequent draw operations will be to the\nrender target until it is replaced by a subsequent call to set_render_target.\nThis function supports render targets created by a render script, or a render target resource."
  parameters {
    name: "render_target"
    doc: "render target to set. Omit it, pass `nil`, or use render.RENDER_TARGET_DEFAULT to set the default render target"
    types: "render_target"
    types: "string"
    types: "hash"
    types: "nil"
    is_optional: true
  }
  parameters {
    name: "options"
    doc: "optional render-target activation options"
    types: "render.set_render_target_options"
    is_optional: true
  }
  examples: "How to set a render target and draw to it and then switch back to the default render target\nThe render target defines the depth/stencil buffers as transient, when set_render_target is called the next time the buffers may be invalidated and allow for optimisations depending on driver support\n\n```lua\nfunction update(self, dt)\n    -- set render target so all drawing is done to it\n    render.set_render_target(self.my_render_target, { transient = { graphics.BUFFER_TYPE_DEPTH_BIT, graphics.BUFFER_TYPE_STENCIL_BIT } } )\n\n    -- draw a predicate to the render target\n    render.draw(self.my_pred)\n\n    -- set default render target. This also invalidates the depth and stencil buffers of the current target (self.my_render_target)\n    --  which can be an optimisation on some hardware\n    render.set_render_target(render.RENDER_TARGET_DEFAULT)\n\nend\n```\n\n```lua\nfunction update(self, dt)\n    -- set render target by a render target resource identifier\n    render.set_render_target(\'my_rt_resource\')\n\n    -- draw a predicate to the render target\n    render.draw(self.my_pred)\n\n    -- reset the render target to the default backbuffer\n    render.set_render_target(render.RENDER_TARGET_DEFAULT)\n\nend\n```"
  language: ""
}
elements {
  type: FUNCTION
  name: "render.set_render_target_size"
  brief: "sets the render target size"
  description: "Sets the render target size for a render target created from\neither a render script, or from a render target resource."
  parameters {
    name: "render_target"
    doc: "render target to set size for"
    types: "render_target"
    types: "string"
    types: "hash"
    is_optional: false
  }
  parameters {
    name: "width"
    doc: "new render target width"
    types: "integer"
    is_optional: false
  }
  parameters {
    name: "height"
    doc: "new render target height"
    types: "integer"
    is_optional: false
  }
  examples: "Resize render targets to the current window size:\n\n```lua\nrender.set_render_target_size(self.my_render_target, render.get_window_width(), render.get_window_height())\nrender.set_render_target_size(\'my_rt_resource\', render.get_window_width(), render.get_window_height())\n```"
  language: ""
}
elements {
  type: FUNCTION
  name: "render.enable_texture"
  brief: "sets a texture to the render state"
  description: "Sets the specified texture handle for a render target attachment or a regular texture\nthat should be used for rendering. The texture can be bound to either a texture unit\nor to a sampler name by a hash or a string.\nA texture can be bound to multiple units and sampler names at the same time,\nthe actual binding will be applied to the shaders when a shader program is bound.\n\nWhen mixing binding using both units and sampler names, you might end up in situations\nwhere two different textures will be applied to the same bind location in the shader.\nIn this case, the texture set to the named sampler will take precedence over the unit.\n\nNote that you can bind multiple sampler names to the same texture, in case you want to reuse\nthe same texture for differnt use-cases. It is however recommended that you use the same name\neverywhere for the textures that should be shared across different materials."
  parameters {
    name: "binding"
    doc: "texture binding, either by texture unit, string or hash for the sampler name that the texture should be bound to"
    types: "number"
    types: "string"
    types: "hash"
    is_optional: false
  }
  parameters {
    name: "handle_or_name"
    doc: "render target or texture handle that should be bound, or a named resource in the \"Render Resource\" table in the currently assigned .render file"
    types: "texture"
    types: "string"
    types: "hash"
    is_optional: false
  }
  parameters {
    name: "buffer_type"
    doc: "optional render-target attachment. Defaults to `graphics.BUFFER_TYPE_COLOR0_BIT`. Depth and stencil attachments must have been created as textures; color attachments beyond the first require a render target with multiple color attachments (up to four are supported)."
    types: "graphics.BUFFER_TYPE"
    is_optional: true
  }
  examples: "```lua\nfunction update(self, dt)\n    -- enable target so all drawing is done to it\n    render.set_render_target(self.my_render_target)\n\n    -- draw a predicate to the render target\n    render.draw(self.my_pred)\n\n    -- disable target\n    render.set_render_target(render.RENDER_TARGET_DEFAULT)\n\n    render.enable_texture(0, self.my_render_target, graphics.BUFFER_TYPE_COLOR0_BIT)\n    -- draw a predicate with the render target available as texture 0 in the predicate\n    -- material shader.\n    render.draw(self.my_pred)\nend\n```\n\n```lua\nfunction update(self, dt)\n    -- enable render target by resource id\n    render.set_render_target(\'my_rt_resource\')\n    render.draw(self.my_pred)\n    render.set_render_target(render.RENDER_TARGET_DEFAULT)\n\n    render.enable_texture(0, \'my_rt_resource\', graphics.BUFFER_TYPE_COLOR0_BIT)\n    -- draw a predicate with the render target available as texture 0 in the predicate\n    -- material shader.\n    render.draw(self.my_pred)\nend\n```\n\n```lua\nfunction update(self, dt)\n    -- bind a texture to the texture unit 0\n    render.enable_texture(0, self.my_texture_handle)\n    -- bind the same texture to a named sampler\n    render.enable_texture(\"my_texture_sampler\", self.my_texture_handle)\nend\n```"
  language: ""
}
elements {
  type: FUNCTION
  name: "render.disable_texture"
  brief: "disables a texture on the render state"
  description: "Disables a texture that has previourly been enabled."
  parameters {
    name: "binding"
    doc: "texture binding, either by texture unit, string or hash that should be disabled"
    types: "texture"
    types: "string"
    types: "hash"
    is_optional: false
  }
  examples: "```lua\nfunction update(self, dt)\n    render.enable_texture(0, self.my_render_target, graphics.BUFFER_TYPE_COLOR0_BIT)\n    -- draw a predicate with the render target available as texture 0 in the predicate\n    -- material shader.\n    render.draw(self.my_pred)\n    -- done, disable the texture\n    render.disable_texture(0)\nend\n```"
  language: ""
}
elements {
  type: FUNCTION
  name: "render.get_render_target_width"
  brief: "retrieve the buffer width from a render target"
  description: "Returns the specified buffer width from a render target."
  returnvalues {
    name: "width"
    doc: "the width of the render target buffer texture"
    types: "integer"
  }
  parameters {
    name: "render_target"
    doc: "render target from which to retrieve the buffer width"
    types: "render_target"
    types: "string"
    types: "hash"
    is_optional: false
  }
  parameters {
    name: "buffer_type"
    doc: "which type of buffer to retrieve the width from\n\n- `graphics.BUFFER_TYPE_COLOR0_BIT`\n- `graphics.BUFFER_TYPE_COLOR[x]_BIT` (x: [0..3], if supported!)\n- `graphics.BUFFER_TYPE_DEPTH_BIT`\n- `graphics.BUFFER_TYPE_STENCIL_BIT`"
    types: "graphics.BUFFER_TYPE"
    is_optional: false
  }
  examples: "```lua\n-- get the width of the render target color buffer\nlocal w = render.get_render_target_width(self.target_right, graphics.BUFFER_TYPE_COLOR0_BIT)\n-- get the width of a render target resource\nlocal w = render.get_render_target_width(\'my_rt_resource\', graphics.BUFFER_TYPE_COLOR0_BIT)\n```"
  language: ""
}
elements {
  type: FUNCTION
  name: "render.get_render_target_height"
  brief: "retrieve a buffer height from a render target"
  description: "Returns the specified buffer height from a render target."
  returnvalues {
    name: "height"
    doc: "the height of the render target buffer texture"
    types: "integer"
  }
  parameters {
    name: "render_target"
    doc: "render target from which to retrieve the buffer height"
    types: "render_target"
    types: "string"
    types: "hash"
    is_optional: false
  }
  parameters {
    name: "buffer_type"
    doc: "which type of buffer to retrieve the height from\n\n- `graphics.BUFFER_TYPE_COLOR0_BIT`\n- `graphics.BUFFER_TYPE_DEPTH_BIT`\n- `graphics.BUFFER_TYPE_STENCIL_BIT`"
    types: "graphics.BUFFER_TYPE"
    is_optional: false
  }
  examples: "```lua\n-- get the height of the render target color buffer\nlocal h = render.get_render_target_height(self.target_right, graphics.BUFFER_TYPE_COLOR0_BIT)\n-- get the height of a render target resource\nlocal w = render.get_render_target_height(\'my_rt_resource\', graphics.BUFFER_TYPE_COLOR0_BIT)\n```"
  language: ""
}
elements {
  type: FUNCTION
  name: "render.clear"
  brief: "clears the active render target"
  description: "Clear buffers in the currently enabled render target with specified value. If the render target has been created with multiple\ncolor attachments, all buffers will be cleared with the same value."
  parameters {
    name: "buffers"
    doc: "table with keys specifying which buffers to clear and values set to clear values. Available keys are:\n\n- `graphics.BUFFER_TYPE_COLOR0_BIT`\n- `graphics.BUFFER_TYPE_DEPTH_BIT`\n- `graphics.BUFFER_TYPE_STENCIL_BIT`"
    types: "table<graphics.BUFFER_TYPE, number|vector4>"
    is_optional: false
  }
  examples: "Clear the color buffer and the depth buffer.\n\n```lua\nrender.clear({[graphics.BUFFER_TYPE_COLOR0_BIT] = vmath.vector4(0, 0, 0, 0), [graphics.BUFFER_TYPE_DEPTH_BIT] = 1})\n```"
  language: ""
}
elements {
  type: FUNCTION
  name: "render.draw"
  brief: "draws all objects matching a predicate"
  description: "Draws all objects that match a specified predicate. An optional constant buffer can be\nprovided to override the default constants. If no constants buffer is provided, a default\nsystem constants buffer is used containing constants as defined in materials and set through\n[ref:go.set] (or [ref:particlefx.set_constant]) on visual components."
  parameters {
    name: "predicate"
    doc: "predicate to draw for"
    types: "render_predicate"
    is_optional: false
  }
  parameters {
    name: "options"
    doc: "optional draw options"
    types: "render.draw_options"
    is_optional: true
  }
  examples: "```lua\nfunction init(self)\n    -- define a predicate matching anything with material tag \"my_tag\"\n    self.my_pred = render.predicate({hash(\"my_tag\")})\nend\n\nfunction update(self, dt)\n    -- draw everything in the my_pred predicate\n    render.draw(self.my_pred)\nend\n```\n\nDraw predicate with constants:\n\n```lua\nlocal constants = render.constant_buffer()\nconstants.tint = vmath.vector4(1, 1, 1, 1)\nrender.draw(self.my_pred, {constants = constants})\n```\n\nDraw with predicate and frustum culling (without near+far planes):\n\n```lua\nlocal frustum = self.proj * self.view\nrender.draw(self.my_pred, {frustum = frustum})\n```\n\nDraw with predicate and frustum culling (with near+far planes):\n\n```lua\nlocal frustum = self.proj * self.view\nrender.draw(self.my_pred, {frustum = frustum, frustum_planes = render.FRUSTUM_PLANES_ALL})\n```"
  language: ""
}
elements {
  type: FUNCTION
  name: "render.draw_debug3d"
  brief: "draws all 3d debug graphics"
  description: "Draws all 3d debug graphics such as lines drawn with \"draw_line\" messages and physics visualization."
  parameters {
    name: "options"
    doc: "optional debug-draw options"
    types: "render.debug_draw_options"
    is_optional: true
  }
  examples: "```lua\nfunction update(self, dt)\n    -- draw debug visualization\n    render.draw_debug3d()\nend\n```"
  language: ""
}
elements {
  type: FUNCTION
  name: "render.set_view"
  brief: "sets the view matrix"
  description: "Sets the view matrix to use when rendering."
  parameters {
    name: "matrix"
    doc: "view matrix to set"
    types: "matrix4"
    is_optional: false
  }
  examples: "How to set the view and projection matrices according to\nthe values supplied by a camera.\n\n```lua\nfunction init(self)\n  self.view = vmath.matrix4()\n  self.projection = vmath.matrix4()\nend\n\nfunction update(self, dt)\n  -- set the view to the stored view value\n  render.set_view(self.view)\n  -- now we can draw with this view\nend\n\nfunction on_message(self, message_id, message)\n  if message_id == hash(\"set_view_projection\") then\n     -- camera view and projection arrives here.\n     self.view = message.view\n     self.projection = message.projection\n  end\nend\n```"
  language: ""
}
elements {
  type: FUNCTION
  name: "render.set_projection"
  brief: "sets the projection matrix"
  description: "Sets the projection matrix to use when rendering."
  parameters {
    name: "matrix"
    doc: "projection matrix"
    types: "matrix4"
    is_optional: false
  }
  examples: "How to set the projection to orthographic with world origo at lower left,\nwidth and height as set in project settings and depth (z) between -1 and 1:\n\n```lua\nrender.set_projection(vmath.matrix4_orthographic(0, render.get_width(), 0, render.get_height(), -1, 1))\n```"
  language: ""
}
elements {
  type: FUNCTION
  name: "render.set_blend_func"
  brief: "sets the blending function"
  description: "Specifies the arithmetic used when computing pixel values that are written to the frame\nbuffer. In RGBA mode, pixels can be drawn using a function that blends the source RGBA\npixel values with the destination pixel values already in the frame buffer.\nBlending is initially disabled.\n\n`source_factor` specifies which method is used to scale the source color components.\n`destination_factor` specifies which method is used to scale the destination color\ncomponents.\n\nSource color components are referred to as (R<sub>s</sub>,G<sub>s</sub>,B<sub>s</sub>,A<sub>s</sub>).\nDestination color components are referred to as (R<sub>d</sub>,G<sub>d</sub>,B<sub>d</sub>,A<sub>d</sub>).\nThe color specified by setting the blendcolor is referred to as (R<sub>c</sub>,G<sub>c</sub>,B<sub>c</sub>,A<sub>c</sub>).\n\nThe source scale factor is referred to as (s<sub>R</sub>,s<sub>G</sub>,s<sub>B</sub>,s<sub>A</sub>).\nThe destination scale factor is referred to as (d<sub>R</sub>,d<sub>G</sub>,d<sub>B</sub>,d<sub>A</sub>).\n\nThe color values have integer values between 0 and (k<sub>R</sub>,k<sub>G</sub>,k<sub>B</sub>,k<sub>A</sub>), where k<sub>c</sub> = 2<sup>m<sub>c</sub></sup> - 1 and m<sub>c</sub> is the number of bitplanes for that color. I.e for 8 bit color depth, color values are between `0` and `255`.\n\nThe blended RGBA values of a pixel comes from the following equations:\n\n- R<sub>d</sub> = min(k<sub>R</sub>, R<sub>s</sub> * s<sub>R</sub> + R<sub>d</sub> * d<sub>R</sub>)\n- G<sub>d</sub> = min(k<sub>G</sub>, G<sub>s</sub> * s<sub>G</sub> + G<sub>d</sub> * d<sub>G</sub>)\n- B<sub>d</sub> = min(k<sub>B</sub>, B<sub>s</sub> * s<sub>B</sub> + B<sub>d</sub> * d<sub>B</sub>)\n- A<sub>d</sub> = min(k<sub>A</sub>, A<sub>s</sub> * s<sub>A</sub> + A<sub>d</sub> * d<sub>A</sub>)\n\nBlend function `(graphics.BLEND_FACTOR_SRC_ALPHA, graphics.BLEND_FACTOR_ONE_MINUS_SRC_ALPHA)` is useful for\ndrawing with transparency when the drawn objects are sorted from farthest to nearest.\nIt is also useful for drawing antialiased points and lines in arbitrary order."
  parameters {
    name: "source_factor"
    doc: "source factor"
    types: "graphics.BLEND_FACTOR"
    is_optional: false
  }
  parameters {
    name: "destination_factor"
    doc: "destination factor"
    types: "graphics.BLEND_FACTOR"
    is_optional: false
  }
  examples: "Set the blend func to the most common one:\n\n```lua\nrender.set_blend_func(graphics.BLEND_FACTOR_SRC_ALPHA, graphics.BLEND_FACTOR_ONE_MINUS_SRC_ALPHA)\n```"
  language: ""
}
elements {
  type: FUNCTION
  name: "render.set_blend_func_separate"
  brief: "sets the blend function with separate factors for color and alpha"
  description: "Sets the blend function with separate blend factors for the color and alpha channels."
  parameters {
    name: "source_factor_color"
    doc: "source color blend factor"
    types: "number"
    is_optional: false
  }
  parameters {
    name: "destination_factor_color"
    doc: "destination color blend factor"
    types: "number"
    is_optional: false
  }
  parameters {
    name: "source_factor_alpha"
    doc: "source alpha blend factor"
    types: "number"
    is_optional: false
  }
  parameters {
    name: "destination_factor_alpha"
    doc: "destination alpha blend factor"
    types: "number"
    is_optional: false
  }
  examples: "Set standard alpha blending with separate alpha:\n\n```lua\nrender.set_blend_func_separate(graphics.BLEND_FACTOR_SRC_ALPHA,\n                               graphics.BLEND_FACTOR_ONE_MINUS_SRC_ALPHA,\n                               graphics.BLEND_FACTOR_ONE,\n                               graphics.BLEND_FACTOR_ONE_MINUS_SRC_ALPHA)\n```"
  language: ""
}
elements {
  type: FUNCTION
  name: "render.set_blend_equation_separate"
  brief: "sets the blend equation with separate equations for color and alpha"
  description: "Sets the blend equation with separate equations for the color and alpha channels."
  parameters {
    name: "equation_color"
    doc: "color blend equation"
    types: "graphics.BLEND_EQUATION"
    is_optional: false
  }
  parameters {
    name: "equation_alpha"
    doc: "alpha blend equation"
    types: "graphics.BLEND_EQUATION"
    is_optional: false
  }
  examples: "Set add for color and reverse subtract for alpha:\n\n```lua\nrender.set_blend_equation_separate(graphics.BLEND_EQUATION_ADD,\n                                   graphics.BLEND_EQUATION_REVERSE_SUBTRACT)\n```"
  language: ""
}
elements {
  type: FUNCTION
  name: "render.set_color_mask"
  brief: "sets the color mask"
  description: "Specifies whether the individual color components in the frame buffer is enabled for writing (`true`) or disabled (`false`). For example, if `blue` is `false`, nothing is written to the blue component of any pixel in any of the color buffers, regardless of the drawing operation attempted. Note that writing are either enabled or disabled for entire color components, not the individual bits of a component.\n\nThe component masks are all initially `true`."
  parameters {
    name: "red"
    doc: "red mask"
    types: "boolean"
    is_optional: false
  }
  parameters {
    name: "green"
    doc: "green mask"
    types: "boolean"
    is_optional: false
  }
  parameters {
    name: "blue"
    doc: "blue mask"
    types: "boolean"
    is_optional: false
  }
  parameters {
    name: "alpha"
    doc: "alpha mask"
    types: "boolean"
    is_optional: false
  }
  examples: "```lua\n-- alpha cannot be written to frame buffer\nrender.set_color_mask(true, true, true, false)\n```"
  language: ""
}
elements {
  type: FUNCTION
  name: "render.set_depth_mask"
  brief: "sets the depth mask"
  description: "Specifies whether the depth buffer is enabled for writing. The supplied mask governs\nif depth buffer writing is enabled (`true`) or disabled (`false`).\n\nThe mask is initially `true`."
  parameters {
    name: "depth"
    doc: "depth mask"
    types: "boolean"
    is_optional: false
  }
  examples: "How to turn off writing to the depth buffer:\n\n```lua\nrender.set_depth_mask(false)\n```"
  language: ""
}
elements {
  type: FUNCTION
  name: "render.set_stencil_mask"
  brief: "sets the stencil mask"
  description: "The stencil mask controls the writing of individual bits in the stencil buffer.\nThe least significant `n` bits of the parameter `mask`, where `n` is the number of\nbits in the stencil buffer, specify the mask.\n\nWhere a `1` bit appears in the mask, the corresponding\nbit in the stencil buffer can be written. Where a `0` bit appears in the mask,\nthe corresponding bit in the stencil buffer is never written.\n\nThe mask is initially all `1`\'s."
  parameters {
    name: "mask"
    doc: "stencil mask"
    types: "number"
    is_optional: false
  }
  examples: "```lua\n-- set the stencil mask to all 1:s\nrender.set_stencil_mask(0xff)\n```"
  language: ""
}
elements {
  type: FUNCTION
  name: "render.set_depth_func"
  brief: "sets the depth test function"
  description: "Specifies the function that should be used to compare each incoming pixel\ndepth value with the value present in the depth buffer.\nThe comparison is performed only if depth testing is enabled and specifies\nthe conditions under which a pixel will be drawn.\n\nThe depth function is initially set to `graphics.COMPARE_FUNC_LESS`."
  parameters {
    name: "func"
    doc: "depth test function, see the description for available values"
    types: "graphics.COMPARE_FUNC"
    is_optional: false
  }
  examples: "Enable depth test and set the depth test function to \"not equal\".\n\n```lua\nrender.enable_state(graphics.STATE_DEPTH_TEST)\nrender.set_depth_func(graphics.COMPARE_FUNC_NOTEQUAL)\n```"
  language: ""
}
elements {
  type: FUNCTION
  name: "render.set_stencil_func"
  brief: "sets the stencil test function"
  description: "Stenciling is similar to depth-buffering as it enables and disables drawing on a\nper-pixel basis. First, GL drawing primitives are drawn into the stencil planes.\nSecond, geometry and images are rendered but using the stencil planes to mask out\nwhere to draw.\n\nThe stencil test discards a pixel based on the outcome of a comparison between the\nreference value `ref` and the corresponding value in the stencil buffer.\n\n`func` specifies the comparison function.\nThe initial value is `graphics.COMPARE_FUNC_ALWAYS`.\n\n`ref` specifies the reference value for the stencil test. The value is clamped to\nthe range [0, 2<sup>n</sup>-1], where n is the number of bitplanes in the stencil buffer.\nThe initial value is `0`.\n\n`mask` is ANDed with both the reference value and the stored stencil value when the test\nis done. The initial value is all `1`\'s."
  parameters {
    name: "func"
    doc: "stencil test function, see the description for available values"
    types: "graphics.COMPARE_FUNC"
    is_optional: false
  }
  parameters {
    name: "ref"
    doc: "reference value for the stencil test"
    types: "number"
    is_optional: false
  }
  parameters {
    name: "mask"
    doc: "mask that is ANDed with both the reference value and the stored stencil value when the test is done"
    types: "number"
    is_optional: false
  }
  examples: "```lua\n-- let only 0\'s pass the stencil test\nrender.set_stencil_func(graphics.COMPARE_FUNC_EQUAL, 0, 1)\n```"
  language: ""
}
elements {
  type: FUNCTION
  name: "render.set_stencil_op"
  brief: "sets the stencil operator"
  description: "The stencil test discards a pixel based on the outcome of a comparison between the\nreference value `ref` and the corresponding value in the stencil buffer.\nTo control the test, call [ref:render.set_stencil_func].\n\nThis function takes three arguments that control what happens to the stored stencil\nvalue while stenciling is enabled. If the stencil test fails, no change is made to the\npixel\'s color or depth buffers, and `sfail` specifies what happens to the stencil buffer\ncontents.\n\n`dppass` and `dpfail` specify the stencil buffer actions depending on whether subsequent\ndepth buffer tests succeed (dppass) or fail (dpfail).\n\nThe initial value for all operators is `graphics.STENCIL_OP_KEEP`."
  parameters {
    name: "sfail"
    doc: "action to take when the stencil test fails"
    types: "graphics.STENCIL_OP"
    is_optional: false
  }
  parameters {
    name: "dpfail"
    doc: "the stencil action when the stencil test passes"
    types: "graphics.STENCIL_OP"
    is_optional: false
  }
  parameters {
    name: "dppass"
    doc: "the stencil action when both the stencil test and the depth test pass, or when the stencil test passes and either there is no depth buffer or depth testing is not enabled"
    types: "graphics.STENCIL_OP"
    is_optional: false
  }
  examples: "Set the stencil function to never pass and operator to always draw 1\'s\non test fail.\n\n```lua\nrender.set_stencil_func(graphics.COMPARE_FUNC_NEVER, 1, 0xFF)\n-- always draw 1\'s on test fail\nrender.set_stencil_op(graphics.STENCIL_OP_REPLACE, graphics.STENCIL_OP_KEEP, graphics.STENCIL_OP_KEEP)\n```"
  language: ""
}
elements {
  type: FUNCTION
  name: "render.set_cull_face"
  brief: "sets the cull face"
  description: "Specifies whether front- or back-facing polygons can be culled\nwhen polygon culling is enabled. Polygon culling is initially disabled.\n\nIf mode is `graphics.FACE_TYPE_FRONT_AND_BACK`, no polygons are drawn, but other\nprimitives such as points and lines are drawn. The initial value for\n`face_type` is `graphics.FACE_TYPE_BACK`."
  parameters {
    name: "face_type"
    doc: "face type"
    types: "graphics.FACE_TYPE"
    is_optional: false
  }
  examples: "How to enable polygon culling and set front face culling:\n\n```lua\nrender.enable_state(graphics.STATE_CULL_FACE)\nrender.set_cull_face(graphics.FACE_TYPE_FRONT)\n```"
  language: ""
}
elements {
  type: FUNCTION
  name: "render.set_polygon_offset"
  brief: "sets the polygon offset"
  description: "Sets the scale and units used to calculate depth values.\nIf `graphics.STATE_POLYGON_OFFSET_FILL` is enabled, each fragment\'s depth value\nis offset from its interpolated value (depending on the depth value of the\nappropriate vertices). Polygon offset can be used when drawing decals, rendering\nhidden-line images etc.\n\n`factor` specifies a scale factor that is used to create a variable depth\noffset for each polygon. The initial value is `0`.\n\n`units` is multiplied by an implementation-specific value to create a\nconstant depth offset. The initial value is `0`.\n\nThe value of the offset is computed as `factor` &times; `DZ` + `r` &times; `units`\n\n`DZ` is a measurement of the depth slope of the polygon which is the change in z (depth)\nvalues divided by the change in either x or y coordinates, as you traverse a polygon.\nThe depth values are in window coordinates, clamped to the range [0, 1].\n\n`r` is the smallest value that is guaranteed to produce a resolvable difference.\nIt\'s value is an implementation-specific constant.\n\nThe offset is added before the depth test is performed and before the\nvalue is written into the depth buffer."
  parameters {
    name: "factor"
    doc: "polygon offset factor"
    types: "number"
    is_optional: false
  }
  parameters {
    name: "units"
    doc: "polygon offset units"
    types: "number"
    is_optional: false
  }
  examples: "```lua\nrender.enable_state(graphics.STATE_POLYGON_OFFSET_FILL)\nrender.set_polygon_offset(1.0, 1.0)\n```"
  language: ""
}
elements {
  type: FUNCTION
  name: "render.get_width"
  brief: "gets the window width, as specified for the project"
  description: "Returns the logical window width that is set in the \"game.project\" settings.\nNote that the actual window pixel size can change, either by device constraints\nor user input."
  returnvalues {
    name: "width"
    doc: "specified window width"
    types: "integer"
  }
  examples: "Get the width of the window.\n\n```lua\nlocal w = render.get_width()\n```"
  language: ""
}
elements {
  type: FUNCTION
  name: "render.get_height"
  brief: "gets the window height, as specified for the project"
  description: "Returns the logical window height that is set in the \"game.project\" settings.\nNote that the actual window pixel size can change, either by device constraints\nor user input."
  returnvalues {
    name: "height"
    doc: "specified window height"
    types: "integer"
  }
  examples: "Get the height of the window\n\n```lua\nlocal h = render.get_height()\n```"
  language: ""
}
elements {
  type: FUNCTION
  name: "render.get_window_width"
  brief: "gets the actual window width"
  description: "Returns the actual physical window width.\nNote that this value might differ from the logical width that is set in the\n\"game.project\" settings."
  returnvalues {
    name: "width"
    doc: "actual window width"
    types: "integer"
  }
  examples: "Get the actual width of the window\n\n```lua\nlocal w = render.get_window_width()\n```"
  language: ""
}
elements {
  type: FUNCTION
  name: "render.get_window_height"
  brief: "gets the actual window height"
  description: "Returns the actual physical window height.\nNote that this value might differ from the logical height that is set in the\n\"game.project\" settings."
  returnvalues {
    name: "height"
    doc: "actual window height"
    types: "integer"
  }
  examples: "Get the actual height of the window\n\n```lua\nlocal h = render.get_window_height()\n```"
  language: ""
}
elements {
  type: FUNCTION
  name: "render.predicate"
  brief: "creates a new render predicate"
  description: "This function returns a new render predicate for objects with materials matching\nthe provided material tags. The provided tags are combined into a bit mask\nfor the predicate. If multiple tags are provided, the predicate matches materials\nwith all tags ANDed together.\n\nThe current limit to the number of tags that can be defined is `64`."
  returnvalues {
    name: "predicate"
    doc: "new predicate"
    types: "render_predicate"
  }
  parameters {
    name: "tags"
    doc: "table of tags that the predicate should match. The tags can be of either hash or string type"
    types: "(string|hash)[]"
    is_optional: false
  }
  examples: "Create a new render predicate containing all visual objects that\nhave a material with material tags \"opaque\" AND \"smoke\".\n\n```lua\nlocal p = render.predicate({hash(\"opaque\"), hash(\"smoke\")})\n```"
  language: ""
}
elements {
  type: FUNCTION
  name: "render.enable_material"
  brief: "enables a material"
  description: "If another material was already enabled, it will be automatically disabled\nand the specified material is used instead.\n\nThe name of the material must be specified in the \".render\" resource set\nin the \"game.project\" setting."
  parameters {
    name: "material_id"
    doc: "material id to enable"
    types: "string"
    types: "hash"
    is_optional: false
  }
  examples: "Enable material named \"glow\", then draw my_pred with it.\n\n```lua\nrender.enable_material(\"glow\")\nrender.draw(self.my_pred)\nrender.disable_material()\n```"
  language: ""
}
elements {
  type: FUNCTION
  name: "render.disable_material"
  brief: "disables the currently enabled material"
  description: "If a material is currently enabled, disable it.\n\nThe name of the material must be specified in the \".render\" resource set\nin the \"game.project\" setting."
  examples: "Enable material named \"glow\", then draw my_pred with it.\n\n```lua\nrender.enable_material(\"glow\")\nrender.draw(self.my_pred)\nrender.disable_material()\n```"
  language: ""
}
elements {
  type: FUNCTION
  name: "render.set_camera"
  brief: "sets the current render camera to be used for rendering"
  description: "Sets the current render camera to be used for rendering. If a render camera\nhas been set by the render script, the renderer will be using its projection and view matrix\nduring rendering. If a projection and/or view matrix has been set by the render script,\nthey will not be used until the current render camera has been reset by calling `render.set_camera()`.\n\nIf the \'use_frustum\' flag in the options table has been set to true, the renderer will automatically use the\ncamera frustum for frustum culling regardless of what frustum is being passed into the render.draw() function.\nNote that the frustum plane option in render.draw can still be used together with the camera."
  parameters {
    name: "camera"
    doc: "camera id to use, or nil to reset"
    types: "url"
    types: "number"
    types: "nil"
    is_optional: false
  }
  parameters {
    name: "options"
    doc: "optional camera options"
    types: "render.camera_options"
    is_optional: true
  }
  examples: "Set the current camera to be used for rendering\n\n```lua\nrender.set_camera(\"main:/my_go#camera\")\nrender.draw(self.my_pred)\nrender.set_camera(nil)\n```\n\nUse the camera frustum for frustum culling together with a specific frustum plane option for the draw command\n\n```lua\n-- The camera frustum will take precedence over the frustum plane option in render.draw\nrender.set_camera(\"main:/my_go#camera\", { use_frustum = true })\n-- However, we can still customize the frustum planes regardless of the camera option!\nrender.draw(self.my_pred, { frustum_planes = render.FRUSTUM_PLANES_ALL })\nrender.set_camera()\n```"
  language: ""
}
elements {
  type: FUNCTION
  name: "render.set_compute"
  brief: "set the current compute program"
  description: "The name of the compute program must be specified in the \".render\" resource set\nin the \"game.project\" setting. If nil (or no arguments) are passed to this function,\nthe current compute program will instead be disabled."
  parameters {
    name: "compute"
    doc: "compute id to use, or nil to disable"
    types: "string"
    types: "hash"
    types: "nil"
    is_optional: false
  }
  examples: "Enable compute program named \"fractals\", then dispatch it.\n\n```lua\nrender.set_compute(\"fractals\")\nrender.enable_texture(0, self.backing_texture)\nrender.dispatch_compute(128, 128, 1)\nrender.set_compute()\n```"
  language: ""
}
elements {
  type: FUNCTION
  name: "render.dispatch_compute"
  brief: "dispatches the currently enabled compute program"
  description: "Dispatches the currently enabled compute program. The dispatch call takes three arguments x,y,z which constitutes\nthe \'global working group\' of the compute dispatch. Together with the \'local working group\' specified in the compute shader\nas a layout qualifier, these two sets of parameters forms the number of invocations the compute shader will execute.\nAn optional constant buffer can be provided to override the default constants. If no constants buffer is provided, a default\nsystem constants buffer is used containing constants as defined in the compute program."
  parameters {
    name: "x"
    doc: "global work group size X"
    types: "integer"
    is_optional: false
  }
  parameters {
    name: "y"
    doc: "global work group size Y"
    types: "integer"
    is_optional: false
  }
  parameters {
    name: "z"
    doc: "global work group size Z"
    types: "integer"
    is_optional: false
  }
  parameters {
    name: "options"
    doc: "optional compute-dispatch options"
    types: "render.dispatch_options"
    is_optional: true
  }
  examples: "```lua\nfunction init(self)\n    local color_params = { format = graphics.TEXTURE_FORMAT_RGBA,\n                           width = render.get_window_width(),\n                           height = render.get_window_height()}\n    self.scene_rt = render.render_target({[graphics.BUFFER_TYPE_COLOR0_BIT] = color_params})\nend\n\nfunction update(self, dt)\n    render.set_compute(\"bloom\")\n    render.enable_texture(0, self.backing_texture)\n    render.enable_texture(1, self.scene_rt)\n    render.dispatch_compute(128, 128, 1)\n    render.set_compute()\nend\n```\n\nDispatch a compute program with a constant buffer:\n\n```lua\nlocal constants = render.constant_buffer()\nconstants.tint = vmath.vector4(1, 1, 1, 1)\nrender.dispatch_compute(32, 32, 32, {constants = constants})\n```"
  language: ""
}
elements {
  type: FUNCTION
  name: "render.set_listener"
  brief: "set render\'s event listener"
  description: "Set or remove the rendering-context event listener."
  parameters {
    name: "callback"
    doc: "A callback that receives all render related events.\nPass `nil` if want to remove listener."
    types: "fun(self:script_instance, event_type:render.CONTEXT_EVENT)"
    types: "nil"
    is_optional: false
  }
  examples: "Set listener and handle render context events.\n\n```lua\n--- custom.render_script\nfunction init(self)\n   render.set_listener(function(self, event_type)\n       if event_type == render.CONTEXT_EVENT_CONTEXT_LOST then\n           --- Some stuff when rendering context is lost\n       elseif event_type == render.CONTEXT_EVENT_CONTEXT_RESTORED then\n           --- Start reload resources, reload game, etc.\n       end\n   end)\nend\n```"
  language: ""
}
info {
  namespace: "render"
  name: "Render"
  brief: "Rendering API documentation"
  description: "Rendering functions, messages and constants. The \"render\" namespace is\naccessible only from render scripts.\n\nThe rendering API was originally built on top of OpenGL ES 2.0, and it uses a subset of the\nOpenGL computer graphics rendering API for rendering 2D and 3D computer\ngraphics. Our current target is OpenGLES 3.0 with fallbacks to 2.0 on some platforms.\n\n[icon:attention] It is possible to create materials and write shaders that\nrequire features not in OpenGL ES 2.0, but those will not work cross platform."
  path: "engine/render/src/render/render_script.cpp"
  file: ""
  language: "Lua"
}
